<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kausambi</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kaushambi, Kosambi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaushambi was an ancient city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a>, about 50 kilometres south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Prayagraj</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the capital of the Vatsa kingdom. One of the great cities of the Buddha's time, ruled by King Udayana, it lay on trade routes between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> and the west and had important Buddhist monasteries such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghositarama">Ghositarama</a>. Its ruins include massive earthen ramparts and a Mauryan stone pillar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kausambi.jpg" alt="Kausambi" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: PHGCOM · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:KausambiCastCopperCoin1stCenturyBCEInscribedKosabi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kosambi, as the city is called in Pali, stood on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamuna">Yamuna</a> upstream of its confluence with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> at Prayaga, a position that made it a centre of trade for the Vatsa kingdom, one of the sixteen mahajanapadas. It was the main halt for traffic coming to Kosala and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> from the south and the west, and Buddhist texts place it thirty leagues by river from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varanasi">Benares</a>. Buddhist commentaries derive its name either from the hermitage of the sage Kusumba or from the neem trees that grew around it.</p><p>Excavations show a fortified town of irregular oblong plan, with gates on the east, west and north sides, bastions, and a moat on three sides still visible in the north. Dense brickwork shows how closely the city was built. A palace excavated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archaeological-survey-of-india">Archaeological Survey of India</a> was built in six phases from the 8th century BCE to the 2nd century CE; its last phase used true arches and was destroyed by fire, leaving a layer of ash.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Occupation may go back to the 12th century BCE. The date of the great rampart is disputed: James Heitzman places it in the 7th to 5th centuries BCE, while G. R. Sharma, who excavated the site in 1949 and from 1951 to 1956, dated it to between 1025 and 955 BCE. In the Buddha's time the city's king was Parantapa, followed by his son Udena, and it held four Buddhist monasteries given by leading citizens, among them the Kukkutārāma and the Pāvārika-ambavana. Tradition says the Buddha spent his ninth rainy season there, and a schism among its monks over a minor rule drove him to retreat alone to the Pārileyyaka forest.</p><p>Kosambi remained an urban centre through the Mauryan and Gupta periods. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>'s schism edict addresses its officials, warning that monks or nuns who break the unity of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangha">Sangha</a> must wear white and live outside it. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pushyamitra-shunga">Pushyamitra Shunga</a> may have moved his capital there. Jain texts place four events in the life of Padmaprabha, the 6th <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankara</a>, at Kauśāmbī and say <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavira">Mahavira</a> visited it five times. More than three thousand stone sculptures have been recovered from the city and nearby sites.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kosambi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Rabatak inscription claims that Kanishka's authority reached cities such as Saketa, <b>Kausambi</b>, Pataliputra and Champa, though how far this meant direct rule rather than recognition is uncertain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kushan-empire">Kushan Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars render it differently: Kangle as the science of the means to artha, <b>Kosambi</b> as the science of material gain, Boesche as the science of political economy and Olivelle as the science of politics.</blockquote><a href="/work/arthashastra">Arthashastra · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Vatsa lay along the Yamuna south-west of modern Prayagraj, with its capital at <b>Kaushambi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-vatsa">Kingdom of Vatsa</a></li><li><blockquote>Tradition says a flood of the Ganges drove the court from Hastinapura to <b>Kaushambi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kuru-kingdom">Kuru Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
